STM32G4: keep the ROM bootloader on USB (USE_ROM_BOOTLOADER_NO_USART)

ST's ROM bootloader on the STM32G4 listens on USART1 (PA9/PA10), USART2
(PA2/PA3) and USART3 (PC10/PC11) as well as USB DFU. The first byte on one of
those USARTs makes it commit to that USART and switch USB off, and Windows then
shows "Unknown USB Device (Device Descriptor Request Failed)". On the T-Motor
Pacer G4 Alpha Analog, the OSD co-processor sends MSP requests on PC11 about
every 36 ms, which is sooner than a host resets a newly attached device, so
`bl` almost never reaches DFU there.

The bootloader resets the GPIO ports as it starts, so the pins can't be held
off from Betaflight, but it keeps RCC_CCIPR. This patch gives the three USARTs
the LSE as kernel clock just before the jump. With the LSE stopped (this
board has no 32 kHz crystal), they never receive a byte, and USB wins. A
system reset clears CCIPR again, so Betaflight's own UARTs are unaffected.
Nothing changes when the LSE is running, or for boards that don't define
USE_ROM_BOOTLOADER_NO_USART.

Tested on the Analog board (STM32G473) on 2026-11-17:
- Betaflight's jump made by hand over SWD: DFU enumerated 7 times out of 7
  with USART3 starved (USART1 too in two of them), and failed 4 times out
  of 4 without.
- Built in, with Betaflight's own `bl`: DFU came up within a second 15
  times out of 15: with an SWD probe attached, with it removed and the
  battery on, and on USB alone. A firmware write through DFU then kept the
  settings.

Applies to Betaflight 2026.6.2. From the root of the Betaflight source:

    patch -p1 < stm32g4-rom-bootloader-no-usart.patch

--- a/src/platform/STM32/system_stm32g4xx.c
+++ b/src/platform/STM32/system_stm32g4xx.c
@@ -110,11 +110,31 @@
     resetHandler_t *resetHandler;
 } isrVector_t;
 
+#ifdef USE_ROM_BOOTLOADER_NO_USART
+// ST's ROM bootloader listens on USART1 (PA9/PA10), USART2 (PA2/PA3) and USART3 (PC10/PC11) as
+// well as USB, and the first byte it receives on one of those USARTs makes it drop USB. A device
+// that talks on those pins on its own, such as an OSD co-processor or a linked receiver, then stops
+// DFU from ever enumerating. The bootloader resets the GPIO ports but keeps RCC_CCIPR, so give the
+// three USARTs the LSE as kernel clock: while the LSE is stopped they never receive a byte.
+static void romBootloaderStopUsarts(void)
+{
+    if (RCC->BDCR & RCC_BDCR_LSERDY) {
+        return;
+    }
+    const uint32_t usartSel = RCC_CCIPR_USART1SEL | RCC_CCIPR_USART2SEL | RCC_CCIPR_USART3SEL;
+    MODIFY_REG(RCC->CCIPR, usartSel, usartSel);
+}
+#endif
+
 static void systemJumpToBootloader(void)
 {
     //DeInit all used peripherals
     HAL_RCC_DeInit();
 
+#ifdef USE_ROM_BOOTLOADER_NO_USART
+    romBootloaderStopUsarts();
+#endif
+
     //Disable all system timers and set to default values
     SysTick->CTRL = 0;
     SysTick->LOAD = 0;
